### Why Is Accuracy Misleading?

The dataset is highly imbalanced. Only approximately 0.17% of transactions are fraudulent.

A model that predicts **every transaction as legitimate** could achieve approximately **99.83% accuracy** while failing to identify any fraudulent transactions.

Therefore, accuracy alone is not a reliable measure of fraud detection performance.

This project focuses on Precision, Recall, F1-Score, and AUC-ROC to provide a more meaningful evaluation.

## ⚖️ Handling Class Imbalance

The project explores techniques to address the imbalance between legitimate and fraudulent transactions.

### SMOTE Oversampling

SMOTE (Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique) generates synthetic examples of the minority class to help the model learn fraud-related patterns.

SMOTE should be applied **only to the training data**, after splitting the dataset. Applying it before the train/test split can cause data leakage and produce misleading evaluation results.

### Class Weighting

Alternatively, models can use `class_weight='balanced'` to assign greater importance to the minority class during training.

The techniques can be compared to determine which provides the best balance between fraud detection and false alarms.

