## 📊 Model Evaluation

Models are evaluated using metrics designed for imbalanced classification.

### Precision

Precision measures the proportion of transactions predicted as fraudulent that are actually fraudulent.

Higher precision means fewer legitimate transactions are incorrectly flagged.

### Recall

Recall measures the proportion of actual fraudulent transactions that the model successfully identifies.

Higher recall means fewer fraudulent transactions are missed.

### F1-Score

The F1-Score combines precision and recall into a single metric using their harmonic mean.

It is useful when both missed fraud and false alarms matter.

### AUC-ROC

The Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve measures how well the model distinguishes between fraudulent and legitimate transactions across different classification thresholds.

Because fraud detection is highly imbalanced, consider also evaluating the **Precision-Recall curve and Average Precision (PR-AUC)**.

### Which Metric Matters Most?

**Recall is especially important when missing a fraudulent transaction is costly.** However, maximizing recall alone can produce too many false positives, inconveniencing legitimate customers and increasing investigation costs.

The best operating point depends on the relative costs of missed fraud and false alarms. Precision, recall, F1-Score, ROC-AUC, and PR-AUC should be considered together.

